{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially after the first week. Apply a downward level shift (≈ -0.5 to -1.5 EUR in days 1–7, widening to ≈ -4 to -10 EUR by days 15–30).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: the model path tends to flatten/turn up while the true series keeps trending down. Add a negative slope correction that increases with horizon (strongest beyond ~day 10).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean-reversion: after declines, the model predicts rebounds to low/mid-70s that do not materialize. Damp mean-reversion strength (reduce upward corrections) and cap rebounds relative to the last observed price.",
    "Overshoot after short-term upticks: when recent history shows a small bounce, the model extrapolates it too far and too long. Shorten the persistence of recent positive returns (faster decay of momentum).",
    "Late-horizon volatility misplacement: the model introduces mid/late-horizon bumps (days ~20–30) whereas realized outcomes remain lower and smoother. Suppress late-horizon oscillations by shrinking deviations toward a declining trend baseline."
  ]
}
